// Farce: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== farce.f01 | feature | real-time
title: By Order Of The Captain
genre: farce + satire
tone: Clockwork, straight-faced and merciless about uniforms
era: 1906 | period
setting: A small garrison town outside the imperial capital | fictional | town
logline: An unemployed shoemaker who cannot get a passport without a job, or a job without a passport, buys a second-hand captain's uniform, stops ten soldiers in the street, and takes over a town hall for an afternoon.
premise: {friedrich} has spent half his life in prison and the other half being moved on. Every office sends him to another office. He has noticed one thing in fifty-seven years: nobody in this country ever asks a uniform for its papers. In a junk shop there is a guards captain's tunic that nearly fits.
synopsis: The film runs in something like real time, from noon to dusk. {friedrich} changes in a station lavatory and comes out a foot taller. He halts a squad returning from the swimming baths, tells the corporal {corporal_klapp} they are under his orders by command of the highest authority, and marches them to the train. He buys them beer and sausages on the way, which no real officer would, and they love him for it. At the town hall he posts sentries, arrests the mayor {burgermeister} for irregularities in the accounts, and asks for the passport office. There is no passport office: this is the wrong kind of town hall. The farce is that the plan has failed in its first ten minutes and cannot be stopped. Everyone helps. The mayor's wife {frau_mayor} insists on accompanying her husband to custody and orders coffee for the captain. The treasurer {rosenkranz} begs to be allowed to close the books properly first and hands over the cash against a receipt, which {friedrich} signs with his former prison governor's name. The police chief, asleep at his desk, is reprimanded and goes home to wash. A councillor telephones the capital to complain about the lack of notice and is told that the army does not explain itself. Each door that opens offers him more authority and less of what he came for. At dusk he sends the mayor to the capital under guard in two cabs, tells the sentries to hold their posts for half an hour, walks to the station, changes in the same lavatory and goes home in third class with four thousand marks and no passport. The coda is brief. He is caught in ten days. The whole empire laughs, the emperor reportedly included; he is pardoned after two years and issued, at last, a passport. The uniform goes to a museum. The corporal is asked at the inquiry why he obeyed, and says with real surprise: he was a captain.
question: What is it that people obey?
themes: the uniform as the man; bureaucracy's circular doors; obedience as a national habit
audience: General; adults who like their farce with teeth
viewpoint: With {friedrich}, who is improvising and frightened under perfect posture
stakes: Prison for life if caught, and the one document that would let him work
conflict: A nobody in borrowed braid against a town that cannot do enough for him, and gives him everything but what he needs
ending: He leaves with the cash box and no passport, is caught, laughed over and pardoned. The state gives him the paper in the end.
experience: Mounting helpless laughter at a machine obeying, and a sting at the corporal's last line
look: Prussian blue and brass in a prim town square; too-long sleeves; doors, corridors and salutes
sound: Boots in step, a town-hall clock striking each quarter, a telephone bell. A brass band rehearsing somewhere throughout.
m: belong | system | order-vs-change | ironic
@ friedrich | Friedrich Schuster | lead | protagonist | human | he/him | 57 | A passport | Without one he cannot work, stay or leave | He has captured the wrong building | Meek, observant, a mimic of authority from long exposure | From vagrant to captain to a celebrity with papers | Barks in borrowed phrases; mutters in his own | A tunic nearly his size, a cap worn regulation-straight, cracked shoes
@ corporal_klapp | Corporal Klapp | supporting | ally | human | he/him | 24 | To do as he is told | It is the whole of his training | The captain bought him a beer | Solid, cheerful, unquestioning | Unchanged; that is the joke | "At your order, Captain" | Wet hair from the baths, rifle, perfect boots
@ burgermeister | Mayor Doctor Lindemann | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 44 | To be arrested correctly | He is a reserve lieutenant and knows the forms | He outranks nobody in a tunic | Pompous, law-abiding to a fault | From outrage to demanding a proper escort | Legalistic, wounded | Frock coat, pince-nez, a chain of office
@ frau_mayor | Frau Lindemann | supporting | foil | human | she/her | 40 | The thing done decently | Appearances are her profession | Her husband is being taken away before lunch | Gracious, steely, hospitable under fire | From hostess to accomplice by good manners | Drawing-room commands | Tea gown, a hat fetched at speed
@ rosenkranz | Treasurer Rosenkranz | supporting | none | human | he/him | 58 | A receipt | Thirty years of balanced books | The cash is leaving | Exact, timid, conscientious | From protest to careful compliance | Figures, to the pfennig | Sleeve guards, a cash box, two pens
~ friedrich - corporal_klapp | false captain and real corporal | an order in the street | a beer, a salute, and "he was a captain"
~ friedrich - burgermeister | impostor and mayor | an arrest | the mayor insisting on being arrested properly
~ friedrich - rosenkranz | impostor and treasurer | a demand for the cash | a scrupulous receipt for stolen money
